WEBVTT

1
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.250
Don't ask me to philosophize, I'm best when I tell stories.

2
00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.610
Dejan Tiago Stanković is the name under which I write.

3
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.230
Whoever I've been, and now I'm a writer.

4
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:47.210
In the 21st century it no longer matters where you're from, what matters is where you feel at home.

5
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.750
In my experience, you feel most at home where you speak the language well.

6
00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.730
I'm lucky, I speak about three languages well, and get by in a couple more,

7
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.750
so I feel quite comfortable both in Lisbon and here,

8
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.190
and in Zagreb, and, say, in London, and in America.

9
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:10.110
I had no intention of doing translation, translation caught up with me.

10
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.330
Because, studying Portuguese, at some point I realized that you learn the spoken language, but you can't write or read.

11
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.350
And then, of course, I started reading: first newspapers, then books,

12
00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.590
and when the first book by Saramago came into my hands,

13
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:31.650
after a couple of pages I understood that it was a great book — he hadn't yet received the Nobel Prize then —

14
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:36.190
but to read it, I practically had to translate it in my head.

15
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:43.310
And so, instead of translating in my head, I sat down at the computer and carefully translated every sentence.

16
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.730
And it turned out well.

17
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:52.810
About seven years later I picked up "Prokleta avlija" and said: let me try this one,

18
00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.110
and I translated, translated, translated, and it turned out well.

19
00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:04.690
It's not by chance, that is, the fact that I — and I noticed this only in hindsight —

20
00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.750
deal only with the identity of people uprooted from their place.

21
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.990
This seems to be my specialty. My first were "Lisbon Stories,"

22
00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:17.030
where in the end all the characters in Lisbon are mostly uprooted people,

23
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.590
mostly immigrants, locals are barely mentioned there.

24
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.690
Or when I'm sitting alone at home and answer the phone, it often happens to me that I answer in the wrong language.

25
00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:32.410
Say, I wake up in the dark and begin talking to someone in the wrong language.

26
00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:41.750
And it was called the Eighth, I studied here only two years, that is, first and second grade,

27
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.590
I was a victim of that very "specialized education."

28
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:50.530
That determined where I'm from. It was the time of new wave and all that.

29
00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.150
A good part of the stories in "Odakle je bila, odatle je nema" were originally written in Portuguese.

30
00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:01.490
All those based on historical documents that I found in the original in Portuguese

31
00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.830
are in fact written in Portuguese.

32
00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:09.970
And these are Portuguese themes: kings of the 14th century, local realities,

33
00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.850
it's pointless to write that originally in Serbian if it's not about us.

34
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:21.150
Even, say, the novel "Estoril," in which many languages are spoken —

35
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:28.890
I would like to have a version where all dialogues are in the language in which they are spoken,

36
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:33.890
that is, when two Romanians speak — in Romanian, when two Frenchmen or someone speaks French — in French,

37
00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.830
and the language in which I narrate could change.

38
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:47.470
I can't say exactly what architecture was for me, because I studied to be an architect out of some inertia,

39
00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:53.930
I don't remember at all when or why I decided to do it, I was a fairly good student in all subjects,

40
00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:59.170
and since I didn't delve deeply into anything, I had good grades everywhere,

41
00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.070
so when the time came to choose, I didn't know what.

42
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:10.150
I left here for the first time in 1989, left for London, that is, that year I finished the course,

43
00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:16.290
there was nothing left for me to attend, and a lot to take exams on, and I kind of left for a summer internship,

44
00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:21.450
because at that time in England under Thatcher there was a big boom and a lot of construction going on.

45
00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:26.430
When I got there, I still caught that wave of big construction, they were still drafting with technical pens.

46
00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.130
I studied a lot of material for this novel to be born.

47
00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:35.310
I read material equivalent to seventeen telephone directories.

48
00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:42.690
And newspapers, and magazines of that era, and all that, listened to radio of that era, recordings, I really made an effort.

49
00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:50.470
The part with the spies was very interesting, because information about spies is rather inaccurate.

50
00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.490
Everyone lies endlessly there, and after the war all the more so.

51
00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:59.150
And it happens, and not rarely, that the same event, from the same day, exists in three versions,

52
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:07.010
as if from three different planets, rather than about one person in one place.

53
00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:11.210
And then things began to boil over here. My friends were still in London,

54
00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:15.850
they didn't tell me that a crisis had started there, they forgot to mention it.

55
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:22.970
And on the second attempt I rush there and discover that I meet my former English colleagues in stores: they're selling clothes.

56
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.750
Everyone lost their jobs. And I was then doing odd jobs in cafés.

57
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.390
We were refugees, I didn't ask for refugee status only because I was ashamed.

58
00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:39.570
Actually I proceeded from the idea that this little Gabi served as the prototype for the Little Prince.

59
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:47.170
It's not out of the question, because Saint-Exupéry at that time was in Portugal, in Estoril, lived in that hotel,

60
00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:50.310
we know how long he stayed there, we know how he arrived, how he left.

61
00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.050
He wrote a lot about his stay there.

62
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.950
He appears in the book as a character.

63
00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:58.190
In the novel his words are his quotes.

64
00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:01.450
But the spy novel wasn't even the main line for me.

65
00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:03.970
The first were Dučić and Crnjanski.

66
00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:11.410
I especially remembered that whole period, and it moved me last year, when there was this migration crisis

67
00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.530
and when I went to talk to Syrian refugees.

68
00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:21.810
They're a decent middle class that went somewhere, and we, unlike them, flew out on planes.

69
00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:29.630
Otherwise they wouldn't have let us in, from my plane most were not let in, although there were no visas then.

70
00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:36.490
Translating books — I translated only the books I loved most in each language.

71
00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:46.920
That is, I translated four or five of my favorite Portuguese books and as many of ours into Portuguese.

72
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:52.040
These are "The Bridge on the Drina," "Prokleta avlija" by Andrić

73
00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:58.100
and the last but no less dear to me novel, "Kad su cvetale tikve" by Dragoslav Mihailović.

74
00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:03.000
When I was in that England, I needed nothing from these people and from this state.

75
00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.760
I was ready for anything, I'll do whatever, just don't touch me.

76
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.760
I was afraid only of the immigration police — that they'd catch me and deport me.

77
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.020
And I got by there, I wasn't a burden to them, I earned.

78
00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.540
They're afraid someone will come to them who will be a burden.

79
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.300
We all earned, no one was on welfare.

80
00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.220
We couldn't, we didn't have the conditions for it.

81
00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.780
And we all got settled, now they're all successful people.

82
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:40.000
My meeting with Saramago happened like this: I translated a book, and then the bombing of Serbia began.

83
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:46.660
And then I simply called him on the phone and asked him to speak out against it, which he did.

84
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.800
And that was exactly a year after he received the Nobel Prize.

85
00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.340
And here it caused a big response, and nowhere else.

86
00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.020
After the bombing they had some meeting

87
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.160
of translators. Saramago chose 5–6 translators.

88
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:05.840
And thanks to that appeal of mine I ended up among those 5–6 translators, otherwise I wouldn't have.

89
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.480
There were serious translators into English, into French, into our language.

90
00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:17.020
And I think I went with them because he's a warm person.

91
00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:23.720
We spent, say, seven days in Toledo at a seminar, where together we translated his plays.

92
00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:27.520
It was an exceptional, unique experience for me, such a thing doesn't happen twice in a lifetime.

93
00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:35.420
I solved my problem in London by getting married.

94
00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:40.140
And like this: I started dating my ex-wife and said: marry me for the papers.

95
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:41.840
She said: all right, and we got married.

96
00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.840
We met on January 1, married sometime in early March, and in February that's 28 days.

97
00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:53.540
A super-fast wedding, and then we had two children and were married, I think, 18 years.

98
00:08:53.540 --> 00:09:04.580
It was for the papers.

99
00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:10.060
We are best friends to this day.

100
00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:19.200
And once in Lisbon there was a meeting of writers, writers from all over Europe, several hundred of them.

101
00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.800
And there were several of ours from the former republics, I got to talking with them,

102
00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.800
and Saramago was led into the hall, he was already an elderly man, they led him through the crowd,

103
00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:35.180
and on the way he bumped into me, apparently saw a familiar face and was happy,

104
00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:40.500
hugged me, kissed me, and I had my three minutes of fame, absolutely deserved.

105
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:49.240
I remember London not at all as the capital of an empire, because we lived in such parts of London,

106
00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.360
I don't know, in Hampstead, in Golders Green,

107
00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.700
Maida Vale, I don't know, then on King's Road, because we lived in different areas that are actually like villages.

108
00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:06.860
Everywhere you have, you know, a neighbor you drink coffee with, everywhere there's some celebrity everyone knows,

109
00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:13.820
I don't know, for a while my local celebrity was Bob Geldof, then a couple of houses from me Freddie Mercury died,

110
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:17.060
in front of the house we had these big... everyone standing with candles.

111
00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:28.180
"Prokleta avlija" is a beautifully told story that practically anyone can understand, a story told very classically,

112
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:36.740
like "The Bridge on the Drina," and translating it isn't hard at all, it's so perfect that there's nothing to intervene in.

113
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:40.780
The better a text is written, the better it translates.

114
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.200
That's why everything I write, I write in one language, and then translate into another,

115
00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.140
and while translating into the other, I polish it.

116
00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:52.080
In the end I always get two works in two languages that differ slightly.

117
00:10:53.300 --> 00:11:03.980
I came back only at 24, when my son, the eldest, had already been born, came back here, because I was a bit ashamed: no degree, but kids,

118
00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:11.320
so I went to the faculty here and quickly sorted everything out — learned some, cried some on the professors' shoulders:

119
00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:18.880
I'm, like, a social case, I have a child, there won't be a degree, and I promised I wouldn't practice this architecture.

120
00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.280
In Serbia, in our conditions, it's impossible to do literature professionally, I think no one does.

121
00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:30.920
Everyone lives off something else, it's absolutely unfeasible, there simply aren't enough readers.

122
00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:37.620
I think several books are published a day, and even if everyone bought the same books, it still wouldn't be enough.

123
00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:49.560
My audience is 25 million at most, until the book is translated.

124
00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:56.300
And when it's translated — in my case these are author's translations — I have, then, another

125
00:11:56.300 --> 00:12:01.640
maybe 200 million from the Portuguese side, all together. Of potential audience,

126
00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:05.600
half of which lives in the jungles of Brazil or in Africa.

127
00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.700
I first visited Lisbon around age 25,

128
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.980
when my son was about to be born, and I liked it there right away.

129
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.160
However, when we left London, we moved to where

130
00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.120
it was most convenient for us at that moment.

131
00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:23.080
And that — we had a small child — was somewhere in the north of Portugal,

132
00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:27.040
an estate, a wine-growing one, that belonged to my late father-in-law,

133
00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.640
such colorful hills, all vineyards.

134
00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:32.680
Even UNESCO took it under protection as something beautiful.

135
00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:37.720
Well, it's all the same as here, only there's more money, and someone who would buy a book here,

136
00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:39.900
but here he has no money, buys it there.

137
00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:45.780
On the other hand, Marisa, a Portuguese singer, more than once on television

138
00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.620
there raved about her trip to Belgrade and talked a lot on these talk shows:

139
00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.460
look, I've just come back from Belgrade.

140
00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:00.680
And I met her once, I was buying some screws in a big store,

141
00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.020
and she was buying something there.

142
00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:05.320
And I said: listen, I'm from Belgrade.

143
00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:08.040
And asked: what was it that delighted you so much?

144
00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.680
She says: what delighted me was that they filled a whole hall for me,

145
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:17.220
and then, when I heard how much a ticket costs and what the average salary is,

146
00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.900
I was proud that I managed to fill a hall.

147
00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:23.660
And we opened a little company there, at first as a language school,

148
00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.660
since Lucia is a language teacher, then we started doing translations

149
00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:31.960
and created a company of about fifteen people, which in the end I left to her.

150
00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.920
Americans say: do what you love, and you won't work a day in your life.

151
00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:40.140
Well, for most of my life I mostly did what I love,

152
00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:44.500
so neither fatigue nor working hours were ever a problem for me.

153
00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:48.380
And second: when you work for yourself, you supposedly have to work less,

154
00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.640
but in fact you work more than for a boss, because after all you work for yourself.

155
00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:56.960
And now, lately, the charm of this free intellectual

156
00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.100
labor that I do, that is, writing books,

157
00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:04.380
is that I do it, basically, as a non-commercial activity.

158
00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.780
That is, I don't hope to earn a single dinar from it,

159
00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:13.440
and that completely liberates me, I don't have to grovel to anyone.

160
00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:23.340
The reason we moved to Lisbon as a whole family

161
00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:28.220
was that in that village our child had already started middle school,

162
00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:33.860
and I realized that maybe it would be better for him to go to some other school,

163
00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:37.520
where there are more kids, where the kids are more diverse.

164
00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.820
It was the right thing, because the kids went through the whole school, secondary school,

165
00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:44.860
from the fourth, fifth grade of primary school onward — they went through everything in Lisbon,

166
00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:50.160
and now one is studying in America, the older one, we're very proud of him,

167
00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:56.040
and the second is still in Portugal, just finished school, and we're proud of him too,

168
00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:57.020
but not because of grades.

169
00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:10.240
If we want better literature, we'd better give the people an education.

170
00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:15.280
Our problem is that people, even when they go abroad —

171
00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:21.200
going abroad isn't going there, studying and coming back here full of knowledge,

172
00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:24.220
and you can't remember where you've been, because you studied so much.

173
00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:28.980
When you go away, I think it's much more important to learn about that society,

174
00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:35.440
understand their norms of behavior, understand their moral values,

175
00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.520
what they stand for, who they are, what they're against, what they're for,

176
00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:45.380
study this and maybe adopt it — if not entirely, then at least partly,

177
00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.960
although, honestly, since these are good, advanced countries, you should learn from them.

178
00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.160
They're ahead of us.

179
00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:58.860
And when you understand that someone is ahead of us, when you understand that you have someone to learn from,

180
00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:04.160
then you learn, there's nothing shameful in it, I changed a thousand of my views,

181
00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:08.740
on a thousand things — from a provincial, to tell the truth, kid,

182
00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:11.420
who hadn't seen half the things that exist in life.

183
00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:14.600
As if we're on two planets, nothing in common with each other, right?

184
00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:24.040
Interestingly, in the end we're all part of the same European culture,

185
00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:31.520
but we're at its extreme poles — to them we're very direct, we're much ruder,

186
00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:36.360
and our manners are unpolished, we don't know how to eat at the table.

187
00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:41.940
And to us they're sluggish, boring, they have no passion.

188
00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:47.520
They don't care about anything except eating on time.

189
00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.140
That, by the way, is the favorite topic in Portuguese society too.

190
00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:56.340
The Portuguese in their own country — they're, first of all, slightly slowed down, because that's the rhythm of life.

191
00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.720
It's just a mentality. No hurry anywhere.

192
00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:04.420
At one o'clock everything closes, everyone goes to eat,

193
00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:07.680
nobody expects anything to happen between one and two.

194
00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:13.680
Nobody picks up the phone, maybe some emergency services work, I don't know, only cafés are open.

195
00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:17.480
They never eat between meals, it's magical.

196
00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:27.300
I'm not a great connoisseur of poetry, and to translate Fernando Pessoa you have to know a lot,

197
00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:35.020
because he was special in that he played four different poets to perfection, or even more.

198
00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:41.340
He was so stylistically diverse,

199
00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.220
and every kind of poetry came to him to perfection.

200
00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:48.980
That's why many consider him the greatest poet of the 20th century.

201
00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:52.640
On the other hand, the Portuguese language is very beautiful for poetry.

202
00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:57.880
And whatever I translate doesn't sound as beautiful as in Portuguese,

203
00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:02.540
and that holds me back a little, but I translate slowly.

204
00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:05.920
Now, whenever I have time,

205
00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:10.640
I take some Brazilian or Portuguese poem that I like and translate it,

206
00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:21.240
put it in a file, and someday maybe I'll publish poems translated in passing.

207
00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:24.140
Lisbon is actually my favorite city.

208
00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:27.520
Few places in the world can have this —

209
00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.880
and I've been to many — that I literally walk somewhere every day and say:

210
00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:33.820
God, how beautiful this is.

211
00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:37.480
Their sky is a bit darker than ours,

212
00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:41.960
a little, because of some winds in the stratosphere,

213
00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.280
and the light over the city seems completely different because of it.

214
00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:51.760
And those colors of theirs, ocher, pink and so on, and next to that blue — it's really beautiful.

215
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:58.100
And the city is terribly, markedly organic, as if it grew by itself, as if it had grown.

216
00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:05.240
That is, one house leans against another, everything somehow props up

217
00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:08.760
the other, to cover the hill.

218
00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.840
Anyway, it's a serious city.

219
00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:15.300
It's been on this spot for three thousand years, three and a half.

220
00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:19.500
It's the oldest continuously, uninterruptedly inhabited place in Europe,

221
00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:25.120
and ahead of it, I don't know, Damascus, Jerusalem, some places in China, India.

222
00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:30.820
Belgrade is perhaps even an older place where people lived, surely so, than Lisbon.

223
00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:33.780
First of all, Homo sapiens arrived here earlier.

224
00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.480
And the last Neanderthals remained there.

225
00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:39.420
But Belgrade has been depopulated more than once.

226
00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.560
But I was born here, and that's a different matter.

227
00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.640
When we were here in front of the school and there was this noise,

228
00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:56.160
I remembered: I lived on Cetinjska Street on the second floor, across from "Politika,"

229
00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:03.500
and at night, in the heat, I open the window and sleep, and under the window stand "Politika" trucks

230
00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:08.260
and rattle all night, loading newspapers for the provinces,

231
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:11.600
and your pillow turns black — it's that polluted there.

232
00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.340
And I sleep best with that.

233
00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:16.680
Today, if someone puts me in such a situation,

234
00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.840
like at a railway station, where there's noise and it stinks

235
00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.680
of exhaust fumes, I immediately get sleepy.

236
00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:26.360
It's different with, say, the smell of these lindens. They were blooming here recently.

237
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.980
Even wherever I go out here where I live, go to the middle of the street —

238
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.840
not a single linden is visible anywhere.

239
00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.520
And you smell it as if you were standing under it.

240
00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:40.780
And Arabic terribly irritates me: I arrive in Cairo, and after three months — four words.

241
00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:44.660
In some other language I'd already be speaking.

242
00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:50.040
There I still haven't been able to count how many consonants they have.

243
00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:54.480
Say, they have about three "h" sounds, for me it's like when you say "č," "ć" and "š" to foreigners.

244
00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:58.040
For them it's all "šešer, šešer, šešer," the same thing.

245
00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:10.100
Home is truly where there's someone to meet you at the airport.

246
00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.100
That is, it's really your true city.

247
00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:17.560
Where you have a network of acquaintances, where someone will volunteer and come to pick you up — that is truly your city.

248
00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.060
I think that's one of the reasons why it's Belgrade.

249
00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:25.760
Even though I tolerate the local climate poorly: now hot, now cold.

250
00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:33.380
Belgrade is still home for me.

251
00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:38.540
With the snow, and the wind, and the košava, and everything else.

252
00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:43.480
When I arrive in a foreign city, I have a whole method.

253
00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:51.460
That is, I stay at least a month in each place.

254
00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:55.880
And now I have 5–6 places around the world where I settle in comfortably like that.

255
00:21:58.060 --> 00:22:02.280
For me the main thing when I arrive in a new city is to first study the neighborhood.

256
00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.380
Usually I stay with friends — with those who are willing to put up with me.

257
00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:16.140
First I probe the neighborhood: where's the bakery, where you can have breakfast, where you can't, then I walk around the neighborhood, I pace these streets,

258
00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:20.100
until I learn where every bench is and so on, and then I gradually widen the circle.

259
00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:24.100
Every language, except Serbian, except ours —

260
00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:27.300
each of them gets a little rusty for me when I'm not in that country for a long time.

261
00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:35.240
And the kids tease me about it especially now: the first, say, week after returning to Portugal I sound as if I've just stepped off a ship.

262
00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:46.640
I sound as if I arrived yesterday, a complete foreigner, our housekeeper from Ukraine sounds better than me.

263
00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:50.620
But after a week all this sorts itself out.

264
00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:54.820
And one of my recipes is to arrive and live the life of your hosts.

265
00:22:55.980 --> 00:23:03.940
Say, I'm going to my brother, who in America basically has a contracting firm, he builds things there.

266
00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:11.540
And all his workers are Mexicans, I like going with him to construction sites, watching how these Mexicans work there, what's what.

267
00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:18.160
Or, say, when I go to Cairo now, a friend lives there, he's the spouse of a diplomat.

268
00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:22.040
He's very bored there, and I'm very welcome.

269
00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:25.140
It's very nice, like in a Russian novel,

270
00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:33.580
when relatives come to stay and sit forever. A cousin came, couldn't get married in her district, so she came and sits for six months.

271
00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:38.540
And waits until she finds a husband. That's how I arrive too and live completely other people's lives.

272
00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:45.560
I also really love these dramas around austerity measures, because even with these austerity measures — considering how much they work — they get paid great.

273
00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:50.900
Really. Here's my opinion. Considering how much they work, they're paid phenomenally.

274
00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:57.000
There everyone works for a six.

275
00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:03.500
A six minus, if possible, just so as not to accidentally get a ten, just so as not to accidentally overwork.

276
00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:11.320
All people in their own country are spoiled. And when you go to another country — all the people I work with are foreigners.

277
00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:17.800
I have this Ukrainian woman, and at the company most are foreigners. Since it's a language firm, it's logical that most were foreigners.

278
00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:30.440
That's what I was in England. Basically cheap labor, but I could work a lot and earned as much as they did, only worked a bit more.

279
00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:42.080
I think for my kids Serbia will be what Vojvodina is for me, where my mother is from.

280
00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:49.140
It's nice for me to go there, I feel some connection with it, but I go rarely.

281
00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:59.340
They're not from here. And it's good that they're not from here.

282
00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:05.660
And all because here there exists a long-term, as if planned, policy of negative selection.

283
00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:10.320
And negative selection doesn't give positive results.

284
00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:17.630
And people here strangle each other, here there's a small puddle and many crocodiles,

285
00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:23.590
some people who perhaps would be more useful to this society here than somewhere abroad,

286
00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:27.250
are, maybe, somewhere abroad, don't fully reveal themselves or at least live decently.

287
00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:33.570
My kids are a bit unlucky: they have our surname

288
00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:38.270
and the kind of appearance by which they stand out among the Portuguese.

289
00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:45.990
And they, it seems to me, at some point ran into a bit of discrimination.

290
00:25:48.850 --> 00:25:53.990
My sons don't feel one hundred percent at home either here or in Lisbon.

291
00:25:53.990 --> 00:26:00.930
And it's interesting how the elder in two years in America quickly turned into an American,

292
00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:11.890
because it's a wonderful culture of the New World, which absorbs all newcomers, which is built on the identity of settlers.

293
00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:18.470
Here people depend heavily on the current political situation, which doesn't improve in any way.

294
00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:28.590
In stable societies it's somehow normal that over the years your life gets better: you earn more, work less, life gets more comfortable.

295
00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:30.210
You do what you love.

296
00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:34.610
In my life everything is according to plan, according to a list.

297
00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:42.390
And now I roughly know where I'm headed, I won't share it with you, but I have a rough idea where I'm going and what I want.

298
00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:51.250
Because it's important to set some upper limits, that is, roughly set a goal,

299
00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.630
and even if you don't reach the goal, it's important to keep going in the same direction.

300
00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:57.290
The problem with old countries like Portugal,

301
00:26:57.290 --> 00:26:59.910
and England too, but Portugal even more,

302
00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:08.410
is this: an old society where old rules of behavior operate, prehistoric, from the 11th–12th century, nothing has changed.

303
00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:16.070
The same families in power, the same people, the same newspapers, the same surnames ever since newspapers have existed.

304
00:27:16.430 --> 00:27:18.150
And they've existed much longer than here.

305
00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:26.510
That is, it's a society where the rules are known, where you've basically done the main thing if you were born lucky.

306
00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:29.810
They even have such an expression: "born well," like "married well."

307
00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:36.350
Art is a completely special reality that exists separately from our reality.

308
00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:48.490
And in this reality the most beautiful thing for me is when I manage, with my dry words, letters on paper, to evoke a feeling in other people.

309
00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:51.990
At the same time it's easier to make a person cry than laugh.

310
00:27:51.990 --> 00:28:00.490
Very often people stay on the first layer and don't notice the lower layers, it depends on the reader.

311
00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:05.450
And very often they discover layers that I myself didn't suspect.